[
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "RESIDENTIAL VACANCY EXCISE TAX (TABOR)",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "type": "linebreak",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "SHALL THE CITY OF BOULDER TAXES BE INCREASED $6,000,000 ANNUALLY (WHICH AMOUNT REPRESENTS ESTIMATED REVENUES IN 2028, THE FIRST FULL FISCAL YEAR OF COLLECTION), AND BY SUCH AMOUNTS RAISED ANNUALLY THEREAFTER, BY IMPOSING A $4,000 TAX ON VACANT HOMES THAT ARE OCCUPIED FOR 183 DAYS OR LESS PER YEAR, WITH SUCH AMOUNT NEVER FALLING BELOW $4,000 BUT INCREASING ANNUALLY IN ACCORDANCE WITH THE DENVER-AURORA-LAKEWOOD CONSUMER PRICE INDEX UP TO, BUT NEVER EXCEEDING $7,000, WITH THE REVENUE FROM SUCH TAX TO BE USED FOR THE PURPOSE OF SUPPORTING CITY SERVICES, INCLUDING: POLICE AND FIRE PROTECTION; PARKS AND RECREATION; TRANSPORTATION AND MAINTENANCE; AND OTHER GENERAL SERVICES IMPORTANT TO THE QUALITY OF LIFE IN THE CITY; AND SHALL THE REVENUES FROM SUCH TAXES AND ANY RELATED EARNINGS BE COLLECTED, RETAINED, AND SPENT AS A VOTER-APPROVED REVENUE CHANGE WITHOUT LIMITATION AND AN EXCEPTION TO THE REVENUE AND SPENDING LIMITS OF ARTICLE X, SECTION 20 OF THE COLORADO CONSTITUTION?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Who put the measure on the ballot?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "City of Boulder Issue 2J was placed on the ballot by the Boulder City Council through ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Ordinance 8759",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://documents.bouldercolorado.gov/WebLink/DocView.aspx?id=202825&dbid=0&repo=LF8PROD2"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ".",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Short Description:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Imposes a $4,000 annual tax on residential properties occupied for 183 days or fewer per year, indexed annually to the Denver-Aurora-Lakewood consumer price index but never below $4,000 or above $7,000. The city estimates $6 million in revenue in 2028, the first full collection year; revenue would support general city services.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Analysis and Commentary:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Support. City of Boulder Issue 2J was placed on the ballot by the Boulder City Council through ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Ordinance 8759",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://documents.bouldercolorado.gov/WebLink/DocView.aspx?id=202825&dbid=0&repo=LF8PROD2"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ".",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Boulder’s vacancy tax came onto the ballot after several years of work by city councilpeople and community organizing. The main community concerns that raised the idea of a vacancy tax are:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Boulder is a town with significant wealth and residents that own homes in Boulder that are used as second homes, and often unoccupied",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Unoccupied homes do not contribute to the local economy through sales taxes, nor provide people to work in the local economy, and kids to attend our schools. A tax is meant to reduce that behavior and encourage owners that are present in the community. Otherwise, the tax goes to support city budgets for public services that the city must provide regardless.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 2
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "With Boulder now hosting the Sundance Film Festival starting in 2027, people in the community have felt additional urgency to address homes that are largely unoccupied and simply treated as luxury investments, which may increase with Sundance’s arrival.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 3
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "list",
    "version": 1,
    "listType": "bullet",
    "start": 1,
    "tag": "ul"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "I appreciated researching the effectiveness of residential vacancy taxes, as I’m somewhat skeptical about whether the new tax would change behavior or raise significant money for the city’s general fund. Some research in Vancouver showed a measurable but small effect:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "It’s difficult to attribute increases in rental units and declines in vacancy to this tax alone, but the city’s calculations show a slight uptick in available housing units, particularly among condominiums and single-family homes. Despite widespread adoption of these regulatory tools to combat housing availability, residents in Vancouver continue to worry about affordability and neighborhood stability caused by vacancies.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Local Vacancy Taxes: A Tool but Not a Panacea",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://itep.org/vacancy-tax-blight-tax-property-tax-tools/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 8,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " — Institute on Taxation and Economic Policy",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "One difference that has concerned me is that the tax as structured is only $4,000 per year, which doesn’t make sense to me compared to rate more variables based on property value (i.e. Vancouver’s 3%). So the effect on a condo worth $400,000 is much greater than the effect on a detached home with $2,000,000. I’m certainly concerned that overhead and management of the tax may make the exercise not very economically efficient.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The City of Boulder projects that the tax will raise $6,000,000 annually, based on a projected 1,500 units subject to the tax at $4,000 per unit. It’s difficult to determine how likely the unit and total revenue estimates are to be correct.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Part of the reason for that uncertainty is because a second ordinance will be needed to implement the tax once voters approve it. Details such as what reasonable exemptions are allowed (i.e. remodels, in the process of trying to rent or sell a property, etc) will need to be implemented next year, and may affect overall taxation rates.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Overall, I would like to see the tax work and effectively raise money or change behavior. I hope that the council is willing to make adjustments or enable future ballot measures to get the details right if needed.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
